# TanStack Start Is Kind of a Big Deal
created: 2026-06-08, last updated: 2026-08-11, [Read more](https://dev.to/erikch/tanstack-start-is-kind-of-a-big-deal-4nec)

TanStack Start doesn't split it this way.
One primitive, `createServerFn`, handles both a GET read and a POST mutation, and you call it the same way from anywhere.
...
import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'
interface GithubUser {
...
const getGithubUser = createServerFn({ method: 'GET' })
.inputValidator((username: string) => username)
.handler(async ({ data: username }): Promise<GithubUser> => {
const res = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}`, {
headers: {
Accept: 'application/vnd.github+json',
// a token here stays on the server, never ships to the client:
// Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN}`,
},
})
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`User "${username}" not found`)
return (await res.json()) as GithubUser
})
...
That `.handler` runs only on the server, so a token never reaches the browser.
...
You can get there with a route handler too, with shared types or a schema validator.
The difference is that TanStack infers the chain for you by default instead of asking you to wire it up.

